$DIR/main.p:17:10: g holds a view of v (v.Slice(), line 14), which was moved into consume (its parameter takes a pluto/vec.Vec[int] by value) at line 16, so g can no longer be used here
    g looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    v.Slice() at line 14. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line
    16 v was moved into consume (its parameter takes a pluto/vec.Vec[int] by
    value), so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value that
    now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use g before line 16, or move v only after the last use of g
    Or move a copy at line 16, so that v keeps its value:
        v.Clone()
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
